<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  IDE default: run the DEEP tier.

  Rider (and VS) can auto-detect a .runsettings in the solution folder; when it does, every test run
  started from the IDE gets DWARF_DEEP=1 and therefore the deep populations - the same counts
  `scripts/housekeeping.ps1 -Deep` and CI's nightly `deep-test` job use. See tests/Shared/DeepTier.cs,
  whose `DeepTier.Enabled` is the ONE reader of the variable.

  WHY THIS FILE IS NOT WIRED INTO THE BUILD, AND MUST NOT BE:
  `dotnet test` does NOT pick a .runsettings up on its own - it needs an explicit settings switch, or
  an MSBuild `<RunSettingsFilePath>` property. That is deliberate. The fast tier's whole contract is a
  ~90-second suite with a ~10% growth cap (round-21 T1/T4), and the deep tier is minutes longer. If
  anyone ever points `<RunSettingsFilePath>` at this file from Directory.Build.props, CI silently
  switches to the deep counts and the cap becomes meaningless without a single line of the gate
  changing. Keep the IDE opt-in and the command line explicit.

  To get the deep tier from the command line, be explicit instead:
      pwsh scripts/housekeeping.ps1 -Deep          (the supported path)
      dotnet test DwarfMapper.NET.sln -s .runsettings

  DELIBERATELY NOT SET HERE - these change what the tests DO, not how much they cover:
    DWARF_GOLDEN_UPDATE / DWARF_SELF_HEAL - make artifact tests REWRITE the artifact instead of
      failing. Enabling them by default would turn a red gate into a silent commit of whatever the
      code currently produces, which is the drift these gates exist to prevent.
    DWARF_BENCH_SMOKE - drives the BenchmarkDotNet smoke leg; minutes of benchmarking is not what
      "run the tests" should mean in an editor.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <EnvironmentVariables>
      <DWARF_DEEP>1</DWARF_DEEP>
    </EnvironmentVariables>
  </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
